{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by instability as he inherited the county while still a minor. His mother acted as regent until he came of age. During his minority the county faced threats from Muslim forces and internal power struggles. Raymond was educated in the court of Jerusalem and developed strong ties with the royal family. His upbringing prepared him for the complex politics of the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a central figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a rival of the powerful House of Ibelin and often clashed with other nobles over land and influence. He was also a close ally of King Amalric I and later served as regent for the young King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was appointed regent in 1174 and held the position for several years. His leadership was tested during the rise of Saladin who united Muslim forces in Egypt and Syria. Raymond tried to negotiate peace with Saladin but his efforts were often undermined by other crusader leaders who distrusted him. His cautious approach was seen by some as cowardly but others viewed it as pragmatic.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a key role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He opposed the aggressive strategy of Guy of Lusignan who was determined to confront Saladin. Raymond warned against rash action and advocated for defensive tactics. His advice was ignored and he was excluded from the final decision to march to Tiberias. The disastrous defeat at Hattin led to the fall of Jerusalem and the collapse of much of the crusader territories. Raymond survived the battle and retreated to Tripoli. He died in 1187 shortly after the defeat. His death marked the end of a long and turbulent reign. Raymond III was remembered as a skilled diplomat but also as a man caught between loyalty and survival in a collapsing world. His legacy remains debated among historians who question whether his caution was wisdom or weakness.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -5.1973900554003194e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0013335392577573657}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.2305197237292305e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.01815054751932621}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -8.583032467868179e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.004293865989893675}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.00964965671300888}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.06348636746406555}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -9.059865078597795e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009301149984821677}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00017808281700126827}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.302042750874534e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409242391586304}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0019913145806640387}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.3112935448589269e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.3841830625315197e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.0023977598175406456}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.001